course: dictionary
lesson: 7769
title: Push: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. To push can mean to move something away from you by pressing it, like pushing a cart. It can also mean to press a button. Check which meaning fits by swapping in your idea and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «Mia said "Okay, no worries" and didn't push.» Here push does not mean moving anything. The clue is that Mia did not try to make anyone do something. The words around the word tell you which meaning to use.
explain: Look at «Mum pushed me to practise.» Pushed means Mum tried hard to make you do something. It is not about hands at all. A push can also be a force, and a bigger push makes things speed up more.
fact: Push means to move something away from you by pressing it.
fact: To push a button means to press it.
fact: A push is a force that moves something away.
fact: To push through or past means to move hard through a crowd.
fact: «No pushing» is a safety rule in queues and at the pool.
exercise: «Mum pushed me to practise.» What does pushed mean here? | Pressed a button | *Tried hard to make someone do something | Moved something away by pressing it | Moved hard through a crowd
exercise: Ella wants to push the lift button. What does push mean here? | A force that moves something away | *To press it | To try hard to make someone do something | To move hard through a crowd
exercise: In science, what happens when you give a thing a bigger push? | It turns into water | *It speeds up more | It stops at once | It stays exactly the same
test: «Mia said "Okay, no worries" and didn't push.» In this sentence, push means | Move a cart away from you | Force your way through a crowd | Press a button | *Try to make someone do something
test: The pool rules say «no pushing». What is the rule about? | Not pressing the lift button | *Not shoving others or forcing your way past them | Not pushing a boat out from the shore | Not swimming without a grown-up watching
exam: «Ella had to push through the crowd to reach the door.» What does push through mean here? | Try to make the crowd do something | Press a button on the door | Spend a lot of money to celebrate | *Move hard through the crowd
